---
code: reserved-namespace-squat
when-fires: a book funcdef coincidentally named `*__<role>` squats the reserved oracle namespace. oracle/reserved.rs lint_book_reserved_names. `{role}` twice.
why: rul24M-bare-dorcism-names: accepted-not-prevented; the disclosure is loud (warnings tune high this era).
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# An ordinary book helper that happens to be named like the role an oracle would own.
hork__predict() { hork --stub; }
hork__predict

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
reserved: warning[reserved-namespace-squat]: Book function `hork__predict` sits
in the `__predict` namespace reserved for oracle functions; Dorc treats it as
ordinary shell and runs it verbatim, but a shipped oracle preamble defining the
same name would collide with it. Rename it to stay clear of `*__predict`.
      [ book.sh:3:1
   3 | hork__predict() { hork --stub; }
     | \___________/
loading: note[role-defined-below-its-sites]: `hork__predict` is defined below 1
of its family's sites, and a definition licenses nothing above itself, so those
sites run as if it were absent -- moving it above them is one cut-and-paste.
      [ book.sh:3:1
   3 | hork__predict() { hork --stub; }
     | \___________/
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=1 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=1 may-alias=0
dorc: decision-digest 0e556b2a35600db1808009e9feda0ea364be80bcce660998b855ed7a6c475a2b

$ dorc-loom --this vars
case: reserved-namespace-squat
{{name}} = "hork__predict"
{{role}} = "__predict"
{{sites}} = "1"
{{v0}} = "book.sh"
{{v1}} = "0"
{{v2}} = "0"
{{v3}} = "0"
{{v4}} = "1"
{{v5}} = "0"
